## Architecture rules

Data flows in one direction; each layer only calls the next one:

```text
argv → cli.rs (clap parses and validates) → main.rs → commands::run → commands/<command>.rs → domain/<topic>.rs
                                                                            │
                                                  stdout ← output.rs ←──────┘      stderr ← tracing logs, errors (main.rs)
```

- **`main.rs`** only wires things together: parse arguments, initialise logging, call `commands::run`, turn the result into an exit code. It is the only place that prints errors and decides exit codes. No logic.
- **`cli.rs`** declares the command tree. Global options live in `GlobalArgs` (`global = true`); each command's arguments struct lives in its command module and is referenced from a `Command` variant. Doc comments are the help text: write them for users.
- **Commands** (`src/commands/<command>.rs`) turn arguments into domain calls and own all I/O of the command: opening files, reading standard input, rendering results to `out: &mut impl Write`. They never call `println!`, `std::process::exit` or read environment variables, and they return `anyhow::Result<()>` with context: `.with_context(|| format!("cannot read {}", path.display()))`.
- **Domain** (`src/domain/<topic>.rs`) is pure logic: it takes values or `impl Read`/`impl BufRead` and returns values or errors. No clap, no printing, no opening files by name, no environment, no process exit. This is where the behaviour is unit-tested.
- **Output:** results go to stdout, and nothing else does. Logs and errors go to stderr. Commands that print data support `--format json` through `output::Format` with stable `snake_case` field names, so scripts can rely on them; the text format is for humans. Write through the buffered `out` handed to the command; `commands::run` flushes it.
- **Errors:** messages are lowercase without a trailing period and name the thing that failed: `error: cannot read notes.txt: No such file or directory (os error 2)`. Exit codes: `0` success (also when the reader closes stdout early, e.g. `| head`), `1` runtime error, `2` invalid usage (clap). Never `unwrap()`/`expect()` on user input or I/O outside tests: a panic is a bug.
- **Logging:** `tracing::{warn, info, debug, trace}!` with structured fields, e.g. `debug!(input = %path.display(), ?counts, "counted")`. Default level `warn`; `-v` info, `-vv` debug, `-vvv` trace, `-q` errors only; a non-empty `RUST_LOG` overrides the flags.
- **Performance:** stream input (`BufRead`, line or chunk at a time) instead of reading whole files; write through the buffered, locked stdout; avoid per-item allocations in hot loops. The release profile uses LTO, one codegen unit and stripped symbols.

## Commands
